# @CHECKER@

A proof checker for the **@CALCULUS@** calculus.

Generated by Eudaimonia, a template for verified SMT proof checkers. This
directory is a complete Lake project and does not depend on the generator: it
can be moved anywhere, or made a repository of its own.

## Layout

```text
install/                 regenerating @CALCULUS@ from its signature
  defs/@CALCULUS@.eo     the Eunoia signature
  defs/@CALCULUS@.eos    what its symbols mean
  defs/smt.eos           the SMT-LIB semantics that is written against
  get-eo-compiler.sh     fetch and build the Eunoia compiler
  install-@CALCLOWER@.sh compile the signature into @CALCULUS@/
scripts/                 build.sh, build-rules.sh, rule-status.sh,
                         check-proof-hygiene.sh, run-ci.sh
docs/                    calculus.md, development.md
test/regress/            proofs to check, and the verdict each should get
lakefile.toml            the package: the @CALCULUS@ library and the @EXE@ executable
lean-toolchain           the pinned Lean version
Main.lean                the executable: read a proof, report a verdict
@CALCULUS@.lean          the root of the library
@CALCULUS@/
  SmtEval.lean           G  the primitives the embedding is evaluated over
  @CHECKER@Term.lean     G  the term datatype of the calculus
  SmtModelDefs.lean      G  what the model semantics is built from
  SmtValueOrder.lean     G  the order on values
  SmtModel.lean          G  the model semantics of SMT-LIB
  Spec.lean              G  @CALCULUS@ <-> SMT-LIB, and satisfiability
  @CHECKER@.lean         G  the core checker
  Parser.lean            G  the operator table for this signature
  Api.lean               H  what the executable does with a file
  ApiChecks.lean         H  each check is the component it stands for
  ApiCorrect.lean        H  the soundness theorem, about the text of a file
  Diagnostics.lean       H  why a run came back incomplete
  Proofs/
    Assumptions.lean     H  the side conditions, and deciding them
    RuleSupport/         H  what every rule statement is written against
    CheckerCore.lean     H  correctness of the core, rule-agnostic
    RuleLemmas.lean      G  the dispatcher over the rules
    Checker.lean         H  the soundness theorem
    Rules/               one file per rule: statement generated, proof yours
```

**G** is generated from the signature and is overwritten by
`install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh`; **H** is hand-written and left alone. Each
file says which it is in its own header. `Proofs/Rules/` is both: a rule's
statement is generated with `sorry` for its proof, the proof goes in that same
file, and a reinstall preserves it.

## Getting started

```bash
install/get-eo-compiler.sh       # once: build the Eunoia compiler
install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh   # signature -> Lean
scripts/build.sh                 # check the result
```

Then [`docs/development.md`](docs/development.md) for the working loop and
[`install/README.md`](install/README.md) for what regeneration does and does not
overwrite.

## What @CALCULUS@ must provide

The correctness development is not neutral about vocabulary: it is stated in
terms of specific operators, referenced as constructors of the `UserOp` enum
that `signature/@CALCULUS@.eo` compiles to. A signature that does not declare
them under these names does not lose a feature — the core proofs stop
typechecking.

| symbol | arity | why the core needs it |
| ------ | ----- | --------------------- |
| `and` | binary | the soundness statement is about the conjunction of the assumptions |
| `imp` | binary | discharging an assumption (`scope`) yields an implication |
| `Bool` | type | every assumption and proven term must have EO type `Bool` |
| `true`, `false` | literals | what a proof entails, and what a refutation reaches |

The right name is necessary but not sufficient: each must also **mean** what
the SMT-LIB semantics says. A signature whose `and` is not conjunction compiles
and then fails to prove.

## Building

```bash
scripts/build.sh
```

That builds the library and the executable. Either alone:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@     # the library
scripts/build.sh @EXE@          # the executable
```

`@CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect` is deliberately outside the default target: it depends
on every per-rule proof, which is far too expensive to build every time. Ask
for it by name, and expect it to take a long time:

```bash
scripts/build.sh @CALCULUS@.ApiCorrect
```

The checks CI runs:

```bash
scripts/run-ci.sh
```

## Running

```bash
lake exe @EXE@ <proof>
```

After a build the executable can be run directly, without Lake:

```bash
./.lake/build/bin/@EXE@ <proof>
```

## Status

After `install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh`, this is a **working checker**: it parses
the Eunoia proof format, runs the calculus, and reports one of three verdicts.
`test/regress/run.sh` exercises all three, and `scripts/run-ci.sh` passes.

What it is not yet is a *verified* checker. Every proof it accepts comes back
`incomplete` rather than `correct`, and that is the design:

- **The soundness theorem is a `sorry`.** `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Checker.lean` holds
  `correct___eo_is_refutation`, and everything above it —
  `@CALCULUS@/ApiCorrect.lean`, which states correctness about the text of a
  file — is already wired to it. Discharging it is what makes the executable\'s
  verdict mean something; no other file has to change.
- **The rule proofs are `sorry` stubs**, one per rule of the signature.
  `scripts/rule-status.sh` counts them and `scripts/build-rules.sh` builds the
  ones that get written.
- **The side conditions model nothing yet.**
  `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Assumptions.lean` says so conservatively, which is why
  every accepted proof is `incomplete`. Writing them is what lets a proof come
  back `correct`.

So the verdict is a live readout of how far the development has got, and it can
only improve — a fresh checker never claims more than it has.
